---
/*
  ACHIEVEMENTS STRIP — restores the round-1 stats-row presentation the
  owner explicitly liked (small uppercase intro label, several big
  numbers side by side, each with one short context line, staggered
  reveal) after round 2's single-fact "capability strips" misread the
  direction. Extended, per the round-3 correction, to repeat that exact
  presentation ONCE PER CASE STUDY within one shared section — each
  block gets its own heading + one-line subline, its own stats, and a
  closing inline link out to where the full story lives.

  ROUND 6 — FINAL styling pass (owner ships tomorrow). Owner verdict on
  round 5's solid dark fills: too dark/muddy, `gold-800` specifically
  read "brownishly bad." Fixed by moving UP the palette scales to
  bright, saturated, opaque fills (brand-300, gold-400, slate-400) —
  still theme-invariant (round 5's proven pattern), but now needing
  DARK text for AA instead of round 5's light text, since the fills
  themselves are light now. See the `.strip-panel-<tone>` comment in
  global.css for the full distance/contrast math.

  COMPOSITION — also per round 6: the owner wants "a smooth and compact
  section," explicitly describing the goal as "one flowing row of
  proof," not "three heavy slabs." Read that as a literal cue, not just
  a metaphor for tighter spacing: the 3 strips now sit in a genuine
  3-column grid at sm+ (stacking to 1 column below that), not a
  vertically stacked list of full-width panels. This is the single
  biggest lever for "more compact" — three panels side-by-side take
  roughly a third of the vertical height three stacked panels do, far
  more than any padding/gap tightening alone could achieve. Within each
  (now much narrower) panel, stats stack in a simple single column
  instead of their own sub-grid — a 3-across sub-grid inside an already
  1/3-width panel would be too cramped to read. Padding, gaps, heading
  size, and stat-value size are all tightened again on top of this
  (see the values below vs round 5's).

  Every stat value must be traceable to a real source (see CLAUDE.md,
  Content honesty) — never invent or round a number to make it look
  better. As of round 6 (the Reception strip's deletion, an explicit
  owner decision — see index.astro's PAGE GOAL comment), there are NO
  placeholder values anywhere on this page; the [OWNER NUMBER NEEDED]
  exception that existed through round 5 no longer applies to anything
  rendered by this component.

  STAT-CELL LOOK — round-1 DNA preserved in spirit (`border-s-2 ...
  ps-4`, extrabold value, staggered `--reveal-delay`) but adapted for
  the narrower per-panel column: the accent bar is a plain dark divider
  (`border-slate-900/20`, flipped from round 5's `border-white/25` now
  that panel fills are light, not dark).

  CASCADE-LAYER NOTE: `.strip-panel-<tone>` is defined after `.strip-
  panel` in global.css (same reasoning as `.card-row`/`.card-compact`)
  so its border-color/background win by source order within the same
  compiled utilities layer. `text-slate-900`/`text-slate-800` on the
  heading/value/description/link are plain Tailwind utilities with no
  competing component-class base setting `color` on those elements —
  no cascade risk stacking them directly. The closing link still uses
  `.focus-ring` + `.focus-ring-on-900` (a small, accepted mismatch
  against non-surface-900 fills, unchanged reasoning from round 5).

  LINK, NOT A STRETCHED CARD: each panel has real content beyond the
  link (heading, subline, several stats) — a plain inline text link at
  the end reads more honestly than making the numbers themselves seem
  clickable. Underlined (since round 5) rather than color-differentiated
  so it doesn't rely on color alone to read as interactive (WCAG 1.4.1);
  round 6 flips the underline/text colors from light (round 5, dark
  fills) to dark (round 6, light fills) — same mechanism, same reason,
  reversed direction, mirroring how the round-3 `.chip-accent-text`
  light-theme hover fix went darker while its dark-theme default went
  lighter for the same underlying reason (resting color already at the
  higher-contrast end needs to move further from it, not closer).

  PROPS:
    intro?  — small label above all strips; doubles as this section's
              <h2> (same pattern as round 2's landmark fix) — omit only
              if the copy genuinely has no intro line.
    strips  — array of case-study blocks:
                heading — case-study label (e.g. "A family
                          fuel-distribution business")
                subline — one line of context under the heading
                stats   — array of { value, description } pairs
                href       — link target (case study or product page)
                linkLabel  — visible link text (e.g. "Read the full story")
                tone?      — "brand" | "gold" | "slate", which panel
                             color this strip gets. Defaults to "brand"
                             if omitted. ("muted"/neutral was removed
                             in round 6 — zero callers once Reception,
                             the only strip using a 4th tone, was cut.)
    note?   — optional small closing line under all strips.
    id?     — optional anchor id.
    flushBottom? — drop this section's bottom border so an immediately
              following section that shares `bg-surface-900` and draws
              its own `border-b` (the HE homepage's QuotesStrip) merges
              with it into one continuous bordered block. Default false
              — the EN homepage keeps the self-contained border-y frame.
*/
interface Stat {
  value:       string;
  description: string;
}

type Tone = "brand" | "gold" | "slate";

interface Strip {
  heading:   string;
  subline:   string;
  stats:     Stat[];
  href:      string;
  linkLabel: string;
  tone?:     Tone;
}

interface Props {
  intro?: string;
  strips: Strip[];
  note?:  string;
  id?:    string;
  flushBottom?: boolean;
}

const { intro, strips, note, id, flushBottom = false } = Astro.props;

// Panel background per tone (global.css). Text/bar/link are shared
// across all tones as of round 6 (fixed dark colors, verified AA
// against every fill at once — see global.css for the numbers).
const TONE_PANEL_CLASS: Record<Tone, string> = {
  brand: "strip-panel strip-panel-brand",
  gold:  "strip-panel strip-panel-gold",
  slate: "strip-panel strip-panel-slate",
};
---

<section id={id} class:list={[flushBottom ? "border-t" : "border-y", "border-border-subtle bg-surface-900 px-6 py-section-sm"]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">

    <!-- Intro label — doubles as the section's <h2> landmark -->
    {intro && (
      <h2 class="reveal text-center text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-text-muted">
        {intro}
      </h2>
    )}

    <!-- THREE-ACROSS ROW OF PROOF (round 6) — a genuine grid, not a stacked list, is the main lever for "compact"; 1 column on mobile, 3 at sm+. -->
    <div class:list={["grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-4", intro && "mt-6"]}>
      {strips.map((strip, i) => {
        const panelClass = TONE_PANEL_CLASS[strip.tone ?? "brand"];
        return (
          <article class:list={["reveal flex flex-col", panelClass]} style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100}`}>

            <!-- Heading bumped text-sm → text-base (owner feedback: "תגדיל את הפונט מעט גם בכותרת של הקייס סטאדיז"). -->
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
              {strip.heading}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-800">
              {strip.subline}
            </p>

            <!-- STATS — stacked single column (each panel is only ~1/3 width now, too narrow for its own sub-grid). -->
            <div class="mt-3 flex flex-col gap-2.5">
              {strip.stats.map((stat, j) => (
                <div
                  class="reveal border-s-2 border-slate-900/20 ps-3 text-start"
                  style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100 + j * 60}`}
                >
                  <p class="text-lg font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-xl">
                    {stat.value}
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-xs leading-snug text-slate-800">
                    {stat.description}
                  </p>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            <!--
              CLOSING LINK — plain inline text link, not a stretched card
              target. Dark, underlined (round 6 — light fills need dark
              text; underline, not color, signals "interactive").
              `py-1 -my-1` widens the tap target a little on mobile
              without adding visible gap (negative margin cancels it).
            -->
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs">
              <a
                href={strip.href}
                class="focus-ring focus-ring-on-900 group -my-1 inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-sm py-1 font-medium text-slate-800 underline decoration-slate-800/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-slate-900 hover:decoration-slate-900/70"
              >
                {strip.linkLabel}
                <!-- rtl:rotate-180 (HE mirror fix): resting arrow must point toward line-start (left in RTL); previously only the hover nudge direction flipped, leaving the glyph pointing the wrong way at rest in RTL. -->
                <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 rtl:rotate-180 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 rtl:group-hover:-translate-x-1" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
                </svg>
              </a>
            </p>

          </article>
        );
      })}
    </div>

    {note && (
      <p class="reveal mt-6 text-center text-xs text-text-muted">
        {note}
      </p>
    )}

  </div>
</section>
